WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
SSH key management becomes easier when teams distinguish the roles keys play.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.520
A user or service private key authenticates a client to a server.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:17.180
A public key listed on the server authorizes the matching private key to access a particular account.

4
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:22.160
A host key helps the client verify that the server it reached is the intended server.

5
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.220
These are different trust relationships, and mixing them conceptually leads to weak controls.

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.620
Different automation keys also serve different purposes.

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:40.720
Git deploy keys, CI/CD runner keys, backup keys, monitoring keys, remote administration keys, and deployment keys should not be treated as interchangeable.

8
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:46.580
A key that can fetch one repository should not automatically be able to administer production systems.

9
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.080
A backup key should not quietly become a general remote access key.

10
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.660
Purpose gives scope meaning.

11
00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:02.500
Each key should have an owner, an approved use case, allowed systems, allowed environments, expected users or automation jobs, and a review path.

12
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.120
Ownership answers who can approve changes or removal.

13
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.700
Scope answers what the key can reach.

14
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.560
Environment separation answers whether the key belongs to development, staging, production, or a specific operational domain.

15
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.380
Without those answers, keys become hard to review and hard to retire.

16
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.220
Trust boundaries matter across environments.

17
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.880
A key used in development automation should not automatically work in production.

18
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.260
A key used by a monitoring job should not work for deployment.

19
00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.360
Host trust should be managed separately from client authentication.

20
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:44.060
Clear distinctions let teams grant the right access for the right workflow without turning every SSH key into a broad, long-lived credential.
